  "title": "CHAPTER 23: ECONOMIC REFORMS IN INDIA (1991 – LPG MODEL)",
  "order": 93,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "uuq9w3q",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO ECONOMIC REFORMS"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The 1991 Economic Reforms were introduced to tackle severe balance of payments crises and structural inefficiencies in the Indian economy.",
        "The LPG Model stands for Liberalisation, Privatisation, and Globalisation.",
        "Liberalisation focused on removing economic controls, tariffs, and industrial licensing restrictions.",
        "Privatisation involved disinvestment in Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) and expanding private sector participation.",
        "Globalisation facilitated the integration of the domestic economy with global markets, capital flows, and international trade.",
        "The reforms culminated in the New Industrial Policy of 1991, fundamentally transforming India's growth trajectory."
      ],
      "id": "w8nvnl4"
    },
